# V. CHORALE FANTASIA  b79-116  (3/4, E-flat major)
# The chorale of movement II returns as a cantus firmus, one note to a
# bar, carried by a different colour in each phrase: trombone (ph.1),
# horns (ph.2), two trombones (ph.3), trumpets and horns at the top of
# the orchestra (ph.4).  Around it the first violins spin an obbligato
# made of the sarabande's rhythm (half + quarter, quarter + half).
# Between phrases: strings alone (b89-90), then woodwinds alone
# (b99-100), where the oboe recalls the sarabande's head over G7 and
# the G-major dominant left hanging since b34 finally resolves (b101).
# Phrase 2 ends with a Phrygian half cadence onto G (iv6 - V in C
# minor) - a shadow of the sarabande.  Phrase 3 climbs, bass rising by
# step, to an A-flat cadence.  Phrase 4 (tutti) sets, against the
# chorale, the SARABANDE's own cadence melody (b13-16: a-flat c |
# b-flat a-flat g | f a-flat d | e-flat) - the same notes that were
# cheated by a deceptive cadence at b16 (bass a-flat b-flat -> C).
# Here the bass goes a-flat b-flat -> E-FLAT (b113-115).  The promise
# is kept - after one beat of silence (b114), the old moment of doubt.
# Then the music exhales at once (b115-116, > p).

